==Strategy==
This stage is very simple on paper, but can be troublesome if played poorly. Spamming area attacker meatshields like [[Crazed Tank Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Manic Eraser Cat]] can take out Croconators consistently. [[Primordial Cat (Rare Cat)|Modern Cat]] with research is amazing for the stage, as she is [[Resistant]] against both Metal and Relic. Croconator also serves as a stepping stone for [[Wave Attack|Wave Attackers]].

==='''Strategy 2 (Cheese)'''===
Start the stage by immediately spamming [[Li'l Tank Cat (Special Cat)|Li'l Eraser Cat]]. Continue spamming Li'l Eraser for the rest of the level. When your Li'l erasers are near the base, a few Croconators should appear. Summon [[Curling Cat (Rare Cat)|Green Shell Cat]] once the Breakerblast cannon is ready and activate the cannon right before Green Shell hits the Croconators. The Croconators should be behind the base and the Li'l Erasers should now be damaging the base. Afterwards, summon attackers like [[Hip Hop Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Can Can Cat]] or [[Swimmer Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Fishman Cat]] to finish the base.

Keep in mind that as the Croconators stand very close to the base, units that are too fast like [[Bahamut Cat (Special Cat)|Awakened Bahamut Cat]] or [[Idi:N (Special Cat)|Idi:N]] might clip or units with piercing attacks like [[Slime Cat (Rare Cat)|Slime Cat]] could kill a croconator and trigger the boss wave.

==Trivia==
*Like all [[Unearthed Artifacts]] stages, A Boat Too Far's Japanese name references an idiom. In this case, the name Norikakatta Dorobune (乗りかかった泥船, "Getting on a Mud Boat") is a play on the idiom ''norikakatta fune'' (乗り掛かった船, "Getting on a boat"), which means getting into a situation that is impossible to back out from. The term ''dorobune'' (泥船) in the stage's name is occasionally used to mean a "ship made of mud" that is bound to sink; a metaphor for a project or organization that is likely to fall apart soon.

